Learn where Java records simplify immutable data carriers and where classes remain better.

## The core idea

A record is a concise Java type for a fixed set of data components. The compiler generates accessors, equality, hash code, and a readable string representation. Records are shallowly immutable: a referenced mutable object can still change.

## A practical way to approach it

Use records for request/response models, simple values, and results returned from a calculation. Add compact constructors to validate invariants. Use a regular class when you need mutable state, complex inheritance, or behavior that does not fit a data carrier.

## Try it in a small project

Create a `StudentScore` record with an ID and score, validate that the score is in range, and write a test for both valid and invalid values. Then compare it with a traditional class to see which communicates the model more clearly.

## Common mistakes to avoid

Do not assume records are automatically safe for every domain model. Validate constructor inputs and consider whether referenced collections should be copied defensively.

## Key takeaway

Keep the first version small, make your assumptions explicit, and verify the result with a repeatable test. For production use, check the current official documentation for the tools and versions you choose.

---
## Record components and generated behaviour

A record declares its data components in the header. The compiler supplies a canonical constructor, accessor methods named after the components, and implementations of `equals`, `hashCode`, and `toString`. This removes repetitive code for simple data carriers.

```java
public record StudentScore(String studentId, int score) {
    public StudentScore {
        if (studentId == null || studentId.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("studentId is required");
        }
        if (score < 0 || score > 100) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("score must be 0..100");
        }
    }
}
```

The compact constructor validates the values before the record instance is created. The generated accessors are `studentId()` and `score()`, not JavaBean-style setters. A record has no setters for its components, but immutability is shallow: if a component refers to a mutable list or map, that object can still be changed unless the constructor defensively copies it.

## Where records fit

Records are useful for values that are primarily data: API response models, calculation results, coordinates, and small domain values. They are not a universal replacement for classes. A regular class may be clearer when an object has identity that changes over time, mutable lifecycle state, complex inheritance requirements, or invariants that need carefully controlled operations.

Be thoughtful when placing records at API boundaries. Serialization frameworks may have version-specific requirements, and exposing an internal record directly can couple a public API to the application's internal model. A dedicated response type can provide a stable contract even when internal implementation changes.

## Exercise and checks

Create a record for a money amount with currency and minor-unit value. Decide how to validate the currency code and whether negative values are allowed for your use case. Write tests for valid input, blank currency, and invalid amounts. If a record contains a list, test that changing the original list after construction does not unexpectedly change the record's contents when defensive copying is required.

**Further reading:** [Java Language Specification: Records](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se21/html/jls-8.html#jls-8.10) and [Oracle Java records guide](https://dev.java/learn/).

Use request validation to keep invalid data out of business logic and databases.

## The core idea

Validation at the API boundary gives callers actionable feedback and keeps downstream code simpler. Bean Validation annotations can express constraints such as required fields, size limits, and numeric ranges.

## A practical way to approach it

Define a request DTO, add constraints, enable validation on the controller argument, and handle validation errors with a consistent response format. Keep domain invariants in the domain layer as well, because not every call enters through the HTTP API.

## Try it in a small project

For a registration endpoint, validate email shape, password length, and required fields, then test missing and malformed inputs. Never return submitted passwords in logs or error responses.

## Common mistakes to avoid

A syntactically valid request can still violate business rules. Validate authorization and domain conditions separately from field-level constraints.

## Key takeaway

Keep the first version small, make your assumptions explicit, and verify the result with a repeatable test. For production use, check the current official documentation for the tools and versions you choose.

---
## Put validation at the request boundary

A request DTO describes the fields an endpoint accepts. Bean Validation annotations express constraints close to those fields, while `@Valid` asks Spring MVC to validate the incoming object before the controller proceeds. This keeps malformed input from spreading through business logic.

```java
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Email;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Size;

public record RegistrationRequest(
    @NotBlank @Email String email,
    @NotBlank @Size(min = 12, max = 100) String password
) {}
```

A controller can accept the DTO with `@Valid`:

```java
@PostMapping("/registrations")
public ResponseEntity<Void> register(
        @Valid @RequestBody RegistrationRequest request) {
    registrationService.register(request);
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).build();
}
```

The exact imports and dependency setup depend on the Spring Boot version. In current Spring Boot projects, add the validation starter and use the Jakarta validation package. Confirm the dependency and version in the official documentation for the project you are building.

## Validation is not the whole security model

Boundary validation checks shape and simple constraints, but domain rules belong in the domain or service layer too. A user may submit a syntactically valid email that is already registered. A quantity may be a valid positive integer but exceed available inventory. Those rules require application state and must be checked where the business operation is performed.

Do not return submitted passwords, authentication tokens, or sensitive internal details in validation messages. Keep error responses consistent and useful: identify the field and the constraint that failed without echoing secrets. Consider request size limits and rate limits as well as field-level checks.

## Test invalid and valid paths

Write tests for a valid request, a missing email, malformed email, blank password, password below the configured length, and a request with an unknown account state. Verify the status code and response structure. Add service-level tests for business rules that annotations cannot express. If the API supports multiple locales, keep error messages stable enough for clients to interpret and localize them.

**Further reading:** [Spring Boot validation documentation](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/io/validation.html) and [Jakarta Bean Validation specification](https://jakarta.ee/specifications/bean-validation/).

Use constructor injection to make dependencies explicit and classes easier to test.

## The core idea

Dependency injection means a class receives the collaborators it needs instead of creating them internally. Constructor injection makes required dependencies visible and supports immutable fields.

## A practical way to approach it

Define a small service interface when it represents a useful boundary, inject it through the constructor, and keep the class focused on one responsibility. Use test doubles for collaborators that would otherwise require a database or network.

## Try it in a small project

A billing service can receive a payment gateway and an invoice repository. In a unit test, provide fake implementations and verify the decisions without making a real payment or database call.

## Common mistakes to avoid

Do not create an interface for every class without a reason. The goal is clear dependencies and replaceable boundaries, not abstraction for its own sake.

## Key takeaway

Keep the first version small, make your assumptions explicit, and verify the result with a repeatable test. For production use, check the current official documentation for the tools and versions you choose.

---
## Keep object creation separate from business logic

Constructor injection makes a class's required collaborators explicit. A service that needs a repository and a payment gateway should receive those dependencies rather than constructing concrete implementations internally. This makes the service easier to test and reduces hidden coupling.

```java
public interface InvoiceRepository {
    void save(Invoice invoice);
}

public interface PaymentGateway {
    PaymentResult charge(Invoice invoice);
}

public class BillingService {
    private final InvoiceRepository invoices;
    private final PaymentGateway payments;

    public BillingService(
            InvoiceRepository invoices,
            PaymentGateway payments) {
        this.invoices = invoices;
        this.payments = payments;
    }

    public PaymentResult pay(Invoice invoice) {
        PaymentResult result = payments.charge(invoice);
        if (result.successful()) {
            invoices.save(invoice);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

The example illustrates dependency boundaries; a real payment flow also needs idempotency, transaction design, reconciliation, and careful handling of uncertain gateway responses. Saving an invoice only after a successful response is not, by itself, a complete financial workflow.

## Constructor injection in Spring

Spring can resolve a bean's constructor dependencies from the application context. When a class has one constructor, Spring does not require `@Autowired` on that constructor. Prefer required dependencies in the constructor rather than field injection because the object cannot be created in an incomplete state.

Keep interfaces where they represent a meaningful boundary, not simply to create an interface for every class. Too many tiny abstractions can make navigation harder. A repository interface may be useful when persistence is a replaceable boundary; a one-method interface with only one implementation and no independent role may add little value.

## Testing without external side effects

A unit test can provide a fake repository and fake payment gateway, then verify how the service responds to success and failure. Do not call a real payment provider in a unit test. Use integration tests with controlled test infrastructure for wiring and persistence behaviour, and the provider's sandbox for gateway-specific flows.

**Further reading:** [Spring Framework dependency injection](https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-collaborators.html) and [Spring Boot testing](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/testing/index.html).

Modern web applications regularly fetch data, save forms, load search results, and communicate with remote services. These operations do not finish immediately. Promises and `async`/`await` let JavaScript represent work that completes later, but reliable code must also handle failure, concurrency, cancellation, and changes in what the user is asking for.

## 1. What a Promise represents

A Promise represents the eventual outcome of an asynchronous operation. It begins pending and eventually becomes fulfilled with a value or rejected with a reason. Once settled, it does not change to the other state.

```js
const responsePromise = fetch('/api/articles');

responsePromise
  .then((response) => {
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
    }
    return response.json();
  })
  .then((articles) => renderArticles(articles))
  .catch((error) => showError(error.message));
```

The explicit status check matters: `fetch()` normally rejects for network-level failures, but an HTTP response such as 404 or 500 still fulfills the Promise. Check `response.ok` when unsuccessful HTTP statuses should be treated as errors.

## 2. Use async and await for readable control flow

An `async` function always returns a Promise. Inside it, `await` pauses that function until the awaited Promise settles; it does not block the entire browser thread.

```js
async function loadArticles() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/articles');
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Could not load articles (${response.status})`);
    }

    const articles = await response.json();
    renderArticles(articles);
  } catch (error) {
    showError(error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unexpected error');
  }
}
```

Use `try/catch` around operations where you can recover, show a helpful message, or add context. Do not use an empty catch block: it hides the reason the operation failed and leaves users guessing.

## 3. Model the user interface as states

A data-driven component usually needs at least four states: idle, loading, success, and error. Treating these as explicit states prevents contradictory UI, such as showing an old success message while a new request is still running.

A simple pattern is:

```js
async function refresh() {
  setState({ status: 'loading', data: null, error: null });

  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/articles');
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('The article list is unavailable');

    const data = await response.json();
    setState({ status: 'success', data, error: null });
  } catch (error) {
    setState({
      status: 'error',
      data: null,
      error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error'
    });
  }
}
```

The `setState` function here is illustrative; the actual implementation depends on whether the project uses React, plain DOM code, or another UI framework. The principle is to make each state visible and testable.

## 4. Run independent operations concurrently

Awaiting two independent requests one after another increases total wait time:

```js
const profile = await loadProfile();
const recommendations = await loadRecommendations();
```

If neither depends on the other, start them together:

```js
const [profile, recommendations] = await Promise.all([
  loadProfile(),
  loadRecommendations()
]);
```

`Promise.all` rejects as soon as one input rejects. Use it when every result is required for the operation to succeed. If each result should be inspected independently, use `Promise.allSettled`:

```js
const results = await Promise.allSettled([
  loadAnnouncements(),
  loadRecommendations(),
  loadLearningStats()
]);

for (const result of results) {
  if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {
    console.log('Loaded:', result.value);
  } else {
    console.error('A section failed:', result.reason);
  }
}
```

In a real interface, show partial results where that is useful rather than failing the entire page because an optional panel could not load.

## 5. Cancel work that is no longer needed

Search boxes often trigger a request for each query. If the user types again before the previous request finishes, the earlier result may arrive last and overwrite the newer result.

Use an `AbortController` to cancel a previous fetch:

```js
let activeController;

async function searchArticles(query) {
  activeController?.abort();
  activeController = new AbortController();

  try {
    const response = await fetch(
      `/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`,
      { signal: activeController.signal }
    );
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Search failed');

    const results = await response.json();
    renderSearchResults(results);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof DOMException && error.name === 'AbortError') {
      return; // A newer search replaced this one.
    }
    showError('Search is temporarily unavailable.');
  }
}
```

Cancellation is not always supported by every asynchronous operation. When it is not, use a request identifier or sequence counter and ignore results that are no longer current.

## 6. Set time limits and handle retries carefully

Some requests can remain pending for longer than the user can reasonably wait. A timeout can stop waiting and allow the interface to recover. Modern environments can combine an abort signal with a timeout, but check browser support for the runtime you target.

Retries are appropriate for some transient failures, such as rate limits or temporary network problems. They are not appropriate for every error. A validation error will not be fixed by sending the same request again. For operations that change data—payments, orders, registrations—use an idempotency strategy before retrying because the first request may have succeeded even if its response was lost.

## 7. Common mistakes to avoid

- **Forgetting HTTP status checks:** a 500 response does not automatically reject a fetch Promise.
- **Awaiting independent tasks sequentially:** use concurrency when all tasks can safely run together.
- **Swallowing errors:** log diagnostic details appropriately and show users a useful message.
- **Updating the UI with stale results:** cancel old requests or verify that the response is still current.
- **Retrying non-idempotent actions blindly:** protect actions against duplicate submission.
- **Leaving loading indicators forever:** always resolve the loading state on success, failure, or cancellation.

## 8. A practical exercise

Build a small search page that requests articles from an endpoint. Show an idle hint before the first search, a loading indicator while a request is pending, results on success, and a useful message on failure. Type quickly to trigger overlapping searches and confirm that older results never replace the newest query. Then simulate a server error and verify that the loading state ends.

## Further reading

- [MDN: Promise](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise)
- [MDN: async function](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function)
- [MDN: Fetch API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API)
- [MDN: AbortController](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController)

## Key takeaways

Promises represent asynchronous outcomes; `async`/`await` makes their control flow easier to follow. Check HTTP statuses, model UI states explicitly, run independent work concurrently, cancel obsolete requests, and make retries safe. Good asynchronous code is not just code that eventually works—it is code that behaves clearly when the network is slow or something fails.

# GPT-6.1 Terra: What More Reliable Computer Use Means for Developers

> **B Tecky analysis · 2026-10-26** — A model release is only useful when teams can measure task success, control permissions and recover from mistakes.

Computer-use models can interpret a screen, choose an action, observe the result and continue. That creates a feedback loop rather than a one-shot text response. For developers, this can help with repetitive browser workflows, QA, form completion and legacy interfaces. It also creates risks: a mistaken click can submit a payment, delete a record or expose data.

A robust implementation separates observation, planning, action and verification. Keep actions scoped to a test account, use allowlists for domains and controls, require confirmation before irreversible operations, and log every action with its result. Do not assume that a model's confidence is proof that a task succeeded.

A useful evaluation set contains representative tasks, edge cases, unexpected pop-ups, slow pages and permission failures. Track completion rate, wrong-action rate, time, recovery rate and human-intervention rate. Compare the same tasks across model versions and browser environments.

```js
async function safeAction(plan, browser, policy) {
  if (!policy.allowedDomains.includes(new URL(plan.url).hostname)) {
    throw new Error("Domain is not allowlisted");
  }
  if (plan.irreversible) {
    return { needsHumanApproval: true, plan };
  }
  const result = await browser.perform(plan.action);
  return { result, verified: await browser.verify(plan.expectedState) };
}
```

Start with read-only workflows such as collecting public product details or checking a staging form. Add write access only after tests demonstrate predictable behavior. For production, use least privilege, rate limits, audit logs and a human approval path.

**What to watch next:** availability, supported environments, latency, pricing, and independent task evaluations. A product announcement is not a substitute for testing it against your own workflow.

